    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d867">

                <pb facs="0723" n="715" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg715"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.00/738: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-05-31T19:00:00+09:00">May 31, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 31—9:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>141. The Hayashi Cabinet resigned this afternoon. The next Cabinet will probably
                    contain a few Ministers taken from the parties, in response to a concerted drive
                    by the parties during the last few days. The decision of resignation followed
                    soon after a strong public statement by Prince Konoe<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d867-f1"/> that the Hayashi Cabinet should have admitted some
                    Ministers taken from the parties immediately after the election. High Japanese
                    authorities attribute the situation to a serious error of political judgment on
                    the part of the Premier.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d867-f1" n="20" xml:id="d867-f1-content">
                <p>President of the Japanese House of Peers.</p>
